Task: Standalone smoke-test proving purerest works as a published jar

Summary: Scaffolded smoke-test/ as a genuinely independent sbt build (own
build.sbt, project/build.properties) deliberately not referenced in the
root build.sbt; confirmed via `sbt projects` at the repo root that it's
never swept into the aggregate build. Wrote PurerestConsumptionSuite
(wraps a stub HttpRoutes[IO] with purerest's ServerMetrics.middleware and
asserts a real http.server.request.duration measurement is recorded via
Metrics.test's in-memory testkit) before adding the purerest dependency —
confirmed Red (fails to compile: org.http4s/purerest unresolved). Added
`libraryDependencies += "io.github.beckfordp" %% "purerest" % purerestVersion`
(purerestVersion read from -DpurerestVersion, never hardcoded since it's
git-derived and changes every commit) — confirmed Green: `sbt
purerest/publishLocal` in the main repo, then `sbt -DpurerestVersion=<version>
test` in smoke-test/ resolves purerest from ~/.ivy2/local and passes.
Added scripts/verify-purerest-consumption.sh automating the whole flow, and
ran it (all checks passed). Reran the main repo's full test suite
(purerest 44/44, inventoryService 10/10, orderService 21/21) to confirm
nothing regressed.

Files changed: smoke-test/build.sbt, smoke-test/project/build.properties,
smoke-test/src/test/scala/smoketest/PurerestConsumptionSuite.scala,
scripts/verify-purerest-consumption.sh

Why: Proves purerest genuinely works when consumed the way a real external
microservice would — an ordinary libraryDependencies entry resolved from a
published jar — not just via this repo's own internal .dependsOn(purerest)
project reference, which is a structurally weaker guarantee.
